            <div class="what-is-text">
                <p class="section-subheading">The Style</p>
                <h2 class="section-heading">What is Barbershop?</h2>
                <div class="section-divider"></div>
                <p>Barbershop harmony is a style of a cappella music characterised by four-part chords that ring in perfect tune — a phenomenon singers call the "overtone" or "fifth voice." No instruments, no backing tracks. Just four voices locking together to create a sound bigger than the sum of its parts.</p>
                <p>The four parts — Tenor, Lead, Baritone, and Bass — each carry a distinct role. The Lead carries the melody, the Bass anchors the chord from below, the Tenor soars above, and the Baritone fills in the harmony that makes the whole chord ring. When all four lock in together, the result is unmistakable.</p>
                <p>Originating in the barbershops of 19th-century America, the style has grown into a worldwide competitive art form, with quartets and choruses performing at festivals and competitions across the globe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="what-is-parts">
                <div class="voice-card">
                    <p class="voice-label">Tenor</p>
                    <p>Sings above the lead, adding brilliance and sparkle to the chord.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="voice-card">
                    <p class="voice-label">Lead</p>
                    <p>Carries the melody. The voice you hum along to.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="voice-card">
                    <p class="voice-label">Baritone</p>
                    <p>Completes the chord — the piece that makes the harmony ring.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="voice-card">
                    <p class="voice-label">Bass</p>
                    <p>Anchors everything from below with a rich, full foundation.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

